# Security Rules

This project is a security-focused demonstration. It is not a claim of HIPAA certification.

Never use real PHI.

All patient data must be synthetic and clearly treated as demo data.

Authorization:

Authentication → user identity → practice/tenant → role → permission → resource check.

Never trust:

- client-supplied practice_id;
- hidden form fields for authorization;
- route visibility as authorization;
- client-side role checks as the only enforcement.

Tenant-owned records must carry or resolve tenant scope server-side.

Sensitive operations should produce audit events where applicable.

Use secure defaults:

- TLS for transport;
- encrypted storage where infrastructure supports it;
- secrets outside source control;
- least privilege;
- rate limiting;
- secure cookies where applicable;
- safe error responses;
- strict input validation;
- upload validation.

Do not implement "certificate pinning" in a browser demo as a fake security feature. Document it as
an infrastructure/client capability that depends on the deployment platform if it is discussed.

Do not claim that using a particular editor, AI coding tool or package makes the application
HIPAA compliant.
